{"type":"text","timestamp":1794363941513,"sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","part":{"id":"prt_1c86ce254001tTBKnFgvMhj3an","messageID":"msg_1c86ccd6e001GhPkUy3qEwlnr3","sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","type":"text","text":"<think>Let me plan the implementation.\n\nBackend:\n- Flask app with SQLite storage under `./data`.\n- Endpoints:\n  - `POST /api/auth/register` — 201\n  - `POST /api/auth/login` — 200 with `{\"token\": ...}`\n  - `POST /api/previews` — fetch page, store preview\n  - `GET /api/previews` — paged list\n  - `GET /api/previews/{id}`\n  - `POST /api/previews/{id}/refresh`\n  - `DELETE /api/previews/{id}`\n  - `GET /api/health`\n\nDependencies: need an HTTP fetch library. `requests` is the obvious choice. Also need HTML parsing — could use `html.parser` from stdlib to avoid extra deps (BeautifulSoup). Let me use stdlib `html.parser` to avoid extra deps, but `requests` needs adding. Actually, I could use `urllib.request` from stdlib to avoid any new dependency. But `requests` is nicer. Let me check if the sandbox can install packages — yes, it says it can install packages from public registries through a read-only cache. But the Docker build will need to install them too. The pip install in Docker will need network access at build time — presumably available.\n\nHmm, but the safest is to use stdlib only for HTTP fetching: `urllib.request` handles redirects and gives final URL. However urllib raises HTTPError for non-2xx status codes. We want to store `status_code`. Using `requests` makes this much easier: `requests.get(url, timeout=..., allow_redirects=True)` gives `response.status_code` and `response.url`.\n\nLet me check if pip install works in this sandbox.\n\nAlso the requirement lock file needs to be updated with hashes. That's annoying but doable — pip download / pip hash. Let me check if there's a pip cache / index available.\n\nLet me first test network access for pip.\n\nAlso for HTML parsing: I'll write a small parser using `html.parser` stdlib to extract title, meta description, og:title, og:description, og:image. That avoids BeautifulSoup dependency. That's quite feasible and robust enough.\n\nActually let me consider using `beautifulsoup4` — cleaner code but adds dependencies and requires lock updates. I'll write my own parser to keep deps minimal — but requests is still valuable. Let me check pip availability.\n\nLet me test.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794363941460,"end":1794363941507}}}
