{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794364131363,"sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","part":{"id":"prt_1c86fc80d0013WlMncDmgGyP2z","messageID":"msg_1c86f7b96001CZ50erIdB7HWUv","sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794364131363,"sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","part":{"id":"prt_1c86fc810001Qs5mP6RWY9s4DY","messageID":"msg_1c86f7b96001CZ50erIdB7HWUv","sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","type":"text","text":"<think>Now, one more consideration: `db.execute` commits after each statement — but in `issue_token` called inside `register` after `db.execute` for the user — fine.\n\nHowever, there's a subtlety with `db.execute` being called within a request that later fails: no transaction rollback. Fine.\n\nAnother: the `previews` insert uses `g.user[\"id\"]`.\n\nNow let me also double check `fetch` handles `response.encoding` — with `stream=True`, `response.encoding` is set from headers (for text/* without charset, requests sets ISO-8859-1). My `_decode` uses utf-8 unless charset is in content-type. Good.\n\nWait: `response.encoding` may be None if no content-type. `_decode(raw, content_type, response.encoding)` — if charset in content-type, uses encoding or utf-8. Good.\n\nAlso `requests` with `stream=True` and `iter_content` — for gzip-encoded responses, requests decodes content-encoding automatically. Good.\n\nOne more: `response.url` after redirects — good.\n\nNow let's also think about `Accept-Encoding` and `requests` default — fine.\n\nPotential issue: `requests.get(..., verify=False)` triggers InsecureRequestWarning. Fine.\n\nNow let me update requirements and test the backend.\n\nrequirements.txt: add requests==2.34.2.\n\nrequirements.lock: needs hashes for requests, urllib3, idna, charset-normalizer, certifi, plus existing. Let me generate it with pip. Best approach: use `pip download` for each pinned package (all wheels + sdist) and compute hashes.\n\nActually `pip-compile --generate-hashes` from pip-tools would produce exactly that format. Let me try installing pip-tools in the venv.\n\nThe lock format in the repo looks like pip-compile output (with \"--hash=sha256:...\" and \"# via\" comments). Let me try pip-tools.\n\nNote the existing lock has click==8.5.0 but our venv installed click 8.5.1 — the lock pins 8.5.0. When I regenerate, the versions may change (click 8.5.1, gunicorn 27.0.0 vs 26.2.0 in the lock). I should keep the existing pins stable where possible and only add new packages (requests + deps). Regenerating with pip-compile might bump click/gunicorn. To minimize churn, I'll generate the lock for the full set but with the existing versions pinned as constraints: create a constraints file with the existing pins and let pip-compile respect them.\n\nLet me try:\n1. `pip install pip-tools` in the venv.\n2. `pip-compile backend/requirements.txt --generate-hashes -o backend/requirements.lock` with constraints.\n\nActually pip-compile respects existing pins in requirements.txt but not in the lock. I'll create /workspace/.tmp-constraints.txt with the pins from the existing lock (click==8.5.0, gunicorn==26.2.0, flask==3.1.3, blinker==1.9.0, itsdangerous==2.2.0, jinja2==3.1.6, markupsafe==3.0.4, werkzeug==3.1.9) and pass `-c`.\n\nLet me do it.</think>\n\nNow let's update the backend dependencies with a proper lock file:","time":{"start":1794364131344,"end":1794364131359}}}
