{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794364169681,"sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","part":{"id":"prt_1c8705dba001ad207Jg3oZWWlX","messageID":"msg_1c8704d6b001axbtxPzw11P1t8","sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794364169681,"sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","part":{"id":"prt_1c8705dbe001FbzPlv91oNsxEF","messageID":"msg_1c8704d6b001axbtxPzw11P1t8","sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","type":"text","text":"<think>The Dockerfile installs `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`. With a constraints file containing hashes... Hmm, pip's `--require-hashes` mode is triggered when the *requirements* file has hashes; constraints with hashes also trigger it. The error says flask==3.1.3 hashes missing — because requirements.txt lists flask==3.1.3 without a hash, and in require-hashes mode every requirement needs a hash.\n\nWait, but the original repo had the same structure: requirements.txt with pins (no hashes) + requirements.lock with hashes as constraints. Did that work? The error arises because pip enables require-hashes mode when the constraints file contains hashes... Let me check the original behavior: with `-c lock` where the lock has hashes, pip treats it as require-hashes mode, requiring all requirements (including from -r files) to have hashes.\n\nHmm, but the original repo's Dockerfile did exactly this and presumably worked. Let me test with the original files to see. Let me check git stash: the original requirements.txt was flask==3.1.3, gunicorn==26.2.0, and lock had hashes. Let me test that combination.\n\nActually the error message lists \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=...\" — meaning pip resolved flask from requirements.txt (no hash) and then found the hash in the constraint. It says hashes are required but missing for some requirements. So it fails.\n\nLet me verify with the original files (git stash my changes temporarily) to see if the original setup also fails. If it fails, then the Docker build with the lock file would fail → so the original repo's Dockerfile would break. Interesting. Letme test.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794364169662,"end":1794364169678}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794364173090,"sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a1c8705a47737599cfe951","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git stash -q && rm -rf /workspace/.testvenv && python3 -m venv /workspace/.testvenv && /workspace/.testvenv/bin/pip install -q -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock 2>&1 | tail -6; git stash pop -q; git status --short","workdir":"/workspace"},"output":"ERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n M backend/app.py\n M backend/requirements.lock\n M backend/requirements.txt\n?? .tmp-constraints.txt\n?? .tmpdl/\n?? backend/auth.py\n?? backend/db.py\n?? backend/fetcher.py\n?? backend/utils.py\n","metadata":{"output":"ERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n M backend/app.py\n M backend/requirements.lock\n M backend/requirements.txt\n?? .tmp-constraints.txt\n?? .tmpdl/\n?? backend/auth.py\n?? backend/db.py\n?? backend/fetcher.py\n?? backend/utils.py\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git stash -q && rm -rf /workspace/.testvenv && python3 -m venv /workspace/.testvenv && /workspace/.testvenv/bin/pip install -q -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock 2>&1 | tail -6; git stash pop -q; git status --short","time":{"start":1794364169670,"end":1794364173073}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c8705dc1001s6d1gFGLvHlY7H","sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","messageID":"msg_1c8704d6b001axbtxPzw11P1t8"}}
