{"type":"text","timestamp":1794364555976,"sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","part":{"id":"prt_1c87642b40011a9zExfMWA6WPr","messageID":"msg_1c8763060001wvo0CWUSOrCl3T","sessionID":"ses_e37936048ffeH8FUfoum56bQ5v","type":"text","text":"<think>The command output appeared but the shell hung because the background process keeps the shell's stdout open. I need to fully detach: redirect all output and use `setsid` / `disown`, and close stdin/stdout/stderr.\n\nLet me use `setsid nohup ... < /dev/null > log 2>&1 &` and then `disown`. The issue is the tool waits for the shell's children. Let me use `setsid` to detach the process group.\n\nActually the standard trick: `setsid cmd > log 2>&1 < /dev/null &` — the child is in a new session so the shell shouldn't wait... but the tool might wait for the pipe to close. Redirecting stdout/stderr to a file should close the pipe. In the previous run I redirected to a file... and it still hung. Hmm, maybe because `&` + `sleep 3` and the shell waits for the background job? The bash tool's persistent shell might wait for all children.\n\nLet me try `setsid` explicitly.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794364555956,"end":1794364555972}}}
